<p>
  This is one of the most asked questions by runners and active adults dealing with Achilles pain. And the most honest answer isn't a simple yes or no — it depends on where you are in your recovery and how you approach it.
</p>

<p>
  Here's the framework that I use with every client who asks this question.
</p>


<h2>The Short Answer</h2>

<p>
  In many cases, you can continue some form of running during Achilles tendinopathy recovery — but the volume, intensity, and timing need to be carefully managed. Complete rest from running is rarely necessary and, as discussed elsewhere on this site, can actually work against recovery by reducing the stimulus your tendon needs to adapt.
</p>

<p>
  The key is monitoring your pain response and making intelligent adjustments, not pushing through regardless.
</p>


<h2>The Decision Framework: Should You Run Today?</h2>


<h3>Check your resting pain level before you go out</h3>

<p>
  Before any run, rate your Achilles pain at rest on a scale of 0-10. If you're sitting at 4/10 or above before you've even put your shoes on, today is not a running day. Give the tendon more time to settle.
</p>


<h3>Set your pain ceiling for the run</h3>

<p>
  During the run, your Achilles discomfort should not exceed 4 out of 10. If it climbs above this, reduce your pace, shorten the run, or stop for the day. Pain during running isn't a stop sign — it's information. Stay within the acceptable range.
</p>


<h3>Monitor the 24-hour response</h3>

<p>
  This is the most important check. After your run, note how the tendon responds over the following 24 hours. If pain is the same or lower than before you ran — good. If it's meaningfully higher (more than 1-2 points above your usual baseline) and stays elevated, the run was too much. Reduce distance or intensity next time.
</p>


<h2>Practical Running Modifications</h2>

<p>
  If you want to keep running while recovering, these modifications reduce tendon demand:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Reduce distance: cut your usual run by 30-50% initially</li>
  <li>Reduce intensity: slow down. Easy-pace running loads the Achilles less than fast running</li>
  <li>Avoid hills: uphill and downhill running significantly increase Achilles load</li>
  <li>Avoid speed work and intervals: high-speed running and sudden direction changes are high-load activities for the Achilles</li>
  <li>Choose softer surfaces: grass or trail running generates less impact than hard road surfaces</li>
  <li>Prioritise footwear: well-cushioned shoes with a modest heel drop (8-10 mm) reduce tendon load compared to flat or minimalist shoes</li>
</ul>


<h2>When You Should Stop Running</h2>

<p>
  There are situations where continuing to run while symptomatic is counterproductive:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Pain consistently above 4-5/10 during running that doesn't settle with pacing adjustments</li>
  <li>Pain that is significantly worse the day after every run and takes more than 24 hours to return to baseline</li>
  <li>Progressive worsening of symptoms over a 2-3 week period despite load modifications</li>
  <li>Suspected insertional tendinopathy that is acutely irritated — this area responds less well to continued loading in the short term</li>
</ul>

<p>
  In these cases, take a short break from running (1-2 weeks), focus on the loading protocol, and then reintroduce running gradually using the framework above.
</p>


<h2>Building Back: The Return-to-Run Framework</h2>

<p>
  If you've taken a break, or if you're introducing running for the first time during recovery, a structured progression is essential. Don't jump back to your previous volume on the first good day.
</p>

<p>
  A simple starter framework:
</p>

<ul>
  <li>Week 1-2: Walk 20-30 min with no pain above 3/10. This is your running foundation.</li>
  <li>Week 3-4: Introduce run-walk intervals — 1 min run, 2 min walk, repeat for 20 min.</li>
  <li>Week 5-6: Extend run segments — 2 min run, 1 min walk. Increase total time.</li>
  <li>Week 7-8: Progress toward continuous running at easy pace for 20-30 min.</li>
  <li>Week 9+: Gradually rebuild to target distance over 3-4 further weeks.</li>
</ul>

<p>
  Adjust based on your 24-hour response at each stage. If a step causes a flare, stay at the previous level for another week.
</p>


<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>


<h3>Will running make my Achilles tendinopathy worse?</h3>

<p>
  Unmanaged running with poorly controlled loads can worsen tendinopathy. But intelligently managed running, guided by pain monitoring and progressive volume, is generally compatible with recovery and in some ways supports it.
</p>


<h3>Should I warm up before running with Achilles pain?</h3>

<p>
  Yes. A 5-10 minute walk before running allows the tendon to accommodate gradually. Many people find tendon pain is worst for the first few minutes of activity, then improves — this is normal. Starting slowly allows you to assess whether conditions are right for the full run.
</p>


<h3>Can I run a race while recovering from Achilles tendinopathy?</h3>

<p>
  This depends entirely on where you are in recovery and the distance involved. A short local fun run during mid-recovery is a different conversation to targeting a marathon. Discuss with a clinician who can assess your specific situation.
</p>


